import json,re
from pathlib import Path
T=[]
def term(name,category,definition,example,caution,guide,labels,source=None,aliases=''):
 id=re.sub('[^a-z0-9]+','-',name.lower()).strip('-')
 T.append(dict(id=id,name=name,category=category,definition=definition,example=example,caution=caution,guide=guide,aliases=aliases,sources=[source] if source else [],diagram=dict(kind='concept',title=name,labels=labels,caption=example)))
term('Repetition','Strength','One completed performance of an exercise movement using the agreed start and finish positions. The counting convention should stay consistent across sessions.','Eight complete presses are eight repetitions.','A partial repetition and a full-range repetition are different tasks even when both are counted as one.','logging',['Start position','One movement','Return / finish'],aliases='rep reps repetitions')
term('Set','Strength','A group of repetitions performed together before a rest or a change of exercise. A set is a container for work, not a fixed amount of training stimulus.','Three sets of eight means three separate groups of eight repetitions.','Twenty easy repetitions and five demanding repetitions can both be one set.','volume',['8 reps','Rest','8 reps'])
term('Working set','Strength','A set intended to provide the main training work rather than only prepare for it. The distinction depends on the session plan, not a universal weight cutoff.','Two lighter practice sets precede three planned working sets.','A very demanding warm-up can still add fatigue even if you exclude it from the working-set total.','warmup',['Practice sets','Working sets','Session record'])
term('Warm-up set','Strength','A preparatory set used to rehearse an exercise and approach the planned working load. It can also reveal whether the setup or planned demand needs adjustment.','An easy machine-press set lets you check the seat position before adding weight.','Warm-up sets do not need to become a second workout.','warmup',['Easy load','Check setup','Approach work'])
term('Load','Strength','The resistance used for an exercise. In a log this often means external mass, but bands, bodyweight, leverage, and machines make the mechanical task more complicated.','A dumbbell press may be logged as 16 kg per hand.','Do not compare two cable stacks as if identical printed weights necessarily mean identical resistance.','logging',['External resistance','Exercise setup','Performed task'])
term('1RM','Strength','One-repetition maximum: the greatest load a person can lift once for a specified exercise and movement standard under the test conditions.','A tested bench-press 1RM applies to that bench-press standard.','It is exercise-specific and can change with technique, fatigue, and testing conditions. Testing is not necessary for every training plan.','progression',['One exercise','One valid repetition','Maximum test load'],source='load',aliases='one rep max one repetition maximum')
term('Estimated 1RM','Strength','A prediction of one-repetition maximum calculated from another performance, such as a submaximal set. The formula and closeness to failure affect the estimate.','A calculator uses load and repetitions to estimate a maximum without a maximal attempt.','A calculated personal record is not the same as actually lifting that load. Estimates from high-repetition sets can be especially uncertain.','trends',['Load + repetitions','Prediction formula','Estimated maximum'],aliases='e1rm estimated one rep max')
term('RIR','Strength','Repetitions in reserve: your estimate of additional repetitions possible before the specified endpoint of the set. It is a subjective estimate.','About 2 RIR means you believe roughly two more comparable repetitions were available.','Accuracy varies. Discomfort and technique changes can alter what you mean by another possible repetition.','effort',['Set completed','~2 reps available','Estimated endpoint'],source='rir',aliases='reps in reserve repetitions in reserve')
term('RPE','Strength','Rating of perceived exertion. It reports subjective effort on a named scale. Strength RPE based on RIR and general aerobic exertion scales are not interchangeable.','In a common lifting convention, RPE 8 corresponds to roughly 2 RIR.','Always identify the scale. A cardio effort of 8/10 does not imply two repetitions remain.','effort',['Name the scale','Rate the effort','Record the context'],source='effort',aliases='rating of perceived exertion effort')
term('Technical failure','Strength','The point at which another repetition cannot be performed within the technique standard you chose. It can occur before you are unable to move the load at all.','You stop a row because you can no longer keep the torso position consistent.','Different people may use different technique standards. State yours when comparing sets.','effort',['Chosen standard','Standard no longer met','Stop the set'])
term('Momentary muscular failure','Strength','In common research usage, the point where the concentric phase cannot be completed despite attempting it under the stated exercise conditions.','You attempt another repetition but cannot complete the lifting phase.','It differs from voluntarily stopping because a set feels difficult. Definitions and testing procedures vary between studies.','effort',['Attempt repetition','Cannot complete lift','Set endpoint'],source='effort',aliases='muscle failure training to failure')
term('Progressive overload','Strength','A principle of increasing or adapting training demand over time as capacity changes. It can involve load, repetitions, sets, range, or another task-relevant variable.','You build repetitions at one load before testing the next available load.','It does not require adding weight every workout. Changes should remain interpretable and manageable.','progression',['Current capacity','Appropriate demand','Reassess over time'],source='acsm')
term('Double progression','Strength','A programming method that progresses repetitions within a chosen range and then increases load when a defined criterion is met.','Work from 8 toward 12 reps, then increase load after meeting your rule.','There are several versions. Specify whether the rule applies to every set or another measure.','progression',['Build repetitions','Meet the rule','Increase load'])
term('Volume','Strength','The amount of training work, expressed using a defined measure such as working sets, repetitions, time, or distance. The unit matters.','Six working sets across two weekly sessions is a set-based volume measure.','The word alone does not tell you whether someone means sets, repetitions, or total external weight moved.','volume',['Choose the unit','Count the work','State the period'])
term('Volume load','Strength','The sum of repetitions multiplied by external load across the sets being counted. It is a bookkeeping measure that does not fully capture training stimulus.','3 × 10 × 40 kg equals 1,200 kg of volume load.','Totals across different exercises, ranges, and machines are not directly equivalent.','volume',['Sets × reps','× external load','Volume-load total'])
term('Frequency','Strength','How often a defined activity occurs during a period. It may describe sessions, exercise exposures, or how often a muscle group is trained.','Three gym visits is three sessions per week, even if a particular exercise appears only twice.','Session frequency and per-muscle frequency are different measures.','volume',['Define the activity','Count occurrences','State the time window'])
term('Hypertrophy','Strength','An increase in muscle size. It is different from an immediate pump, short-term swelling, or a change in scale weight alone.','Repeated resistance training can produce increases in muscle size over time.','A larger volume-load total does not directly measure hypertrophy.','volume',['Repeated training','Adaptation over time','Muscle size'],source='load')
term('Strength','Strength','The ability to produce force under specified conditions. In a gym it is often assessed by performance on a particular movement and loading task.','Improved bench-press performance describes strength in that tested task.','Skill, setup, and movement standard influence test results. Strength is not one universal number.','progression',['Specified task','Force production','Measured performance'],source='load')
term('Power','Strength','The rate of doing mechanical work. It relates to force and movement velocity, so producing force quickly matters.','Moving a load faster at a given force changes mechanical power.','A heavier but much slower repetition is not automatically a more powerful one.','concurrent',['Force','× velocity','Mechanical power'])
term('Muscular endurance','Strength','The ability to sustain or repeat muscular work over a task. The outcome depends on the exercise, resistance, duration, and test rules.','The number of valid repetitions at a fixed load can be one endurance measure.','It is not identical to maximal strength or cardiorespiratory endurance.','progression',['Defined load','Repeated work','Task duration'])
term('Compound exercise','Strength','An exercise involving movement at more than one joint. The term describes movement organization, not automatic superiority over other exercises.','A squat involves motion at the hips, knees, and ankles.','Muscle contribution depends on technique, range, load, and anatomy.','rest',['Multiple joints','Coordinated movement','One exercise'])
term('Isolation exercise','Strength','An exercise emphasizing movement at one joint, often used to focus work on a smaller set of muscles. Other muscles may still stabilize the task.','A knee-extension machine primarily trains a knee-extension action.','“Isolation” does not mean only one muscle is active.','volume',['One main joint action','Targeted movement','Supporting stabilization'])
term('Unilateral exercise','Strength','An exercise performed with one side at a time or with a deliberately asymmetric emphasis. Log sides clearly.','Ten lunges per side is different from ten alternating lunges in total.','Do not silently switch between per-side and total-repetition conventions.','logging',['Left-side work','Right-side work','Clear counting rule'])
term('Bilateral exercise','Strength','An exercise performed with both sides acting together in the main movement. The label does not imply perfectly equal contribution from each side.','A conventional two-leg squat is a bilateral exercise.','The total external load is not necessarily divided equally between limbs.','logging',['Both sides','Shared movement','Total task'])
term('Concentric','Strength','A muscle action in which the active muscle shortens while producing force. In many lifts it corresponds to the lifting phase.','The elbow flexors shorten during the upward phase of a curl.','“Up” is not a universal definition for every muscle or exercise.','range',['Active muscle','Shortening','Force production'])
term('Eccentric','Strength','A muscle action in which the active muscle lengthens while producing force. It often controls the lowering phase of a lift.','The elbow flexors lengthen while controlling the lowering of a curl.','It is active control, not simply letting a weight fall.','range',['Active muscle','Lengthening','Controlled force'])
term('Isometric','Strength','A muscle action producing force without a meaningful change in the joint position being held. Internal muscle and tendon behavior can still be complex.','Holding a fixed position against resistance is an isometric task.','A still-looking body does not mean no muscular effort is occurring.','range',['Fixed position','Force maintained','Time under effort'])
term('Range of motion','Strength','The movement excursion used at a joint or in an exercise. It can be described with angles or repeatable practical landmarks.','A row recorded from full arm reach to a specified handle endpoint has a defined range.','The useful range depends on the task and person; a diagram cannot set an individual pain-free limit.','range',['Start landmark','Movement excursion','End landmark'],source='rom',aliases='ROM')
term('Partial repetition','Strength','A repetition intentionally or unintentionally using only part of a defined movement range. Which part is trained matters.','A partial rep might use the lower half or upper half of an exercise.','Do not assume every partial range has the same effect or compare it silently with full-range work.','range',['Defined full range','Selected portion','Recorded partial'],source='rom')
term('Tempo','Strength','The timing of phases within a repetition, often written as a sequence for lowering, pauses, and lifting. The order should be explained.','A note of “3-second lowering” is clearer than an unexplained four-digit code.','The same total repetition time can be distributed across phases in different ways.','logging',['Lowering','Pause / transition','Lifting'])
term('Rest interval','Strength','The time between defined bouts of work, most commonly between sets. State where timing begins and ends.','Two minutes from finishing one set to starting the next is a two-minute inter-set rest.','Rest between exercises and rest between repetitions are different quantities.','rest',['Set ends','Recovery interval','Next set starts'],source='rest')
term('Superset','Strength','Two exercises performed in sequence with little or no planned rest between them, followed by the specified recovery. Arrangements vary.','A press followed by a row can be one superset pairing.','Pairing does not make the exercises physiologically independent; fatigue can carry across.','rest',['Exercise A','Exercise B','Planned recovery'])
term('Autoregulation','Strength','Adjusting a training prescription using current performance or feedback instead of relying only on a fixed plan. The decision rule should be explicit.','Reduce the load if the warm-up and first set are substantially harder than the planned effort.','It does not mean changing the whole session based on one vague mood or device score.','deload',['Planned task','Current feedback','Defined adjustment'])
term('Specificity','Strength','The principle that adaptations and performance improvements relate to the tasks and demands practiced. Transfer between tasks is not unlimited.','Practice on one exercise may improve its performance more than an unrelated movement.','Specificity does not mean variation is useless; it means the comparison should match the goal.','plateau',['Goal task','Relevant practice','Task-specific outcome'])
term('Plateau','Strength','A period without clear improvement in a chosen outcome across comparable observations. The observation window and measurement error matter.','Several comparable sessions show no change in repetitions at a fixed load.','One difficult session does not establish a plateau.','plateau',['Comparable records','Stable outcome','Review the context'])
term('Aerobic exercise','Conditioning','Activity that substantially uses aerobic energy production and challenges cardiorespiratory function over time. Energy systems overlap rather than switching completely on or off.','Brisk walking or sustained cycling can be aerobic activity.','A movement is not automatically moderate or vigorous for every person.','cardio',['Sustained movement','Oxygen-supported energy','Cardiorespiratory demand'],source='activity',aliases='cardio endurance')
term('Moderate intensity','Conditioning','A level of aerobic effort that is meaningfully above easy activity. Relative intensity considers the person, while absolute intensity uses a standardized task measure.','In the talk test, you can talk but would have difficulty singing.','A fixed walking speed may feel very different to two people.','intensity',['Conversation possible','Singing difficult','Relative effort'],source='intensity')
term('Vigorous intensity','Conditioning','A higher level of aerobic effort. In the talk test, speech is limited to a few words before pausing for breath.','A hard uphill effort may make full-sentence conversation difficult.','Vigorous does not mean maximal, and symptoms should not be ignored to preserve a target.','intensity',['Harder breathing','Few words at a time','Higher effort'],source='intensity')
term('Talk test','Conditioning','A practical method of estimating relative aerobic intensity from how comfortably you can speak during activity.','Comfortable conversation versus only a few words helps distinguish effort levels.','It is a rough cue, not a direct lactate or ventilatory-threshold measurement.','intensity',['Move','Try speaking','Interpret effort'],source='intensity')
term('Heart-rate zone','Conditioning','A band of heart rates defined by a chosen model. Models may use maximum heart rate, heart-rate reserve, or measured thresholds.','Two apps can assign different zone boundaries to the same person.','The label “zone 2” is incomplete without the system and settings behind it.','intensity',['Choose a model','Set its boundaries','Interpret the band'])
term('Heart-rate reserve','Conditioning','The difference between maximum and resting heart rate. A reserve-based target adds a chosen fraction of that difference to resting heart rate.','If maximum is 180 and resting is 60 bpm, reserve is 120 bpm.','Both input values can be uncertain. This example is arithmetic, not your training target.','intensity',['180 bpm maximum','− 60 bpm resting','120 bpm reserve'])
term('Maximum heart rate','Conditioning','The highest heart rate reached under appropriate maximal testing conditions. Age-based formulas predict population averages rather than a precise individual value.','A formula-generated maximum and a measured test maximum are different inputs.','Do not treat an age formula as an exact safety boundary or self-test instruction.','intensity',['Test or estimate','Individual variation','Model input'])
term('MET','Conditioning','Metabolic equivalent of task: a standardized expression of energy expenditure relative to a conventional resting reference.','An activity listed at 4 METs has a standardized intensity classification.','MET tables are not individualized measurements of your calorie expenditure.','intensity',['Resting reference','Task multiple','Standardized estimate'],source='intensity',aliases='metabolic equivalent')
term('VO2 max','Conditioning','Maximum rate of oxygen uptake during an appropriate test, often expressed in mL/kg/min. Wearable values are estimates from algorithms.','A laboratory gas-analysis test and a watch estimate use different methods.','Compare methods and conditions before interpreting a small change as adaptation.','wearables',['Oxygen uptake','Maximal test','Rate of consumption'])
term('Pace','Conditioning','Time taken per unit of distance, such as minutes per kilometer. It is the inverse framing of speed.','5:00 min/km corresponds to 12 km/h.','A lower pace number means faster movement when distance units are unchanged.','logging',['5 min','Per 1 km','12 km/h equivalent'])
term('Interval training','Conditioning','Training that alternates defined work periods with recovery periods. Duration, intensity, repetitions, and recovery all define the session.','Six work bouts separated by easier movement form an interval structure.','Intervals are not automatically maximal-effort sprints.','cardio',['Work period','Recovery period','Repeat as planned'])
term('Concurrent training','Conditioning','A program that includes both aerobic and strength training over the same period. The arrangement and total demand affect practical results.','Two strength sessions plus two cycling sessions in a week is concurrent training.','Combining modes does not automatically prevent strength or muscle gains.','concurrent',['Strength work','Aerobic work','Shared schedule'],source='concurrent')
term('Interference effect','Conditioning','A potential reduction in a specific adaptation when training demands are combined compared with a relevant single-mode program. It is outcome- and context-dependent.','Explosive performance may respond differently from muscle size in mixed programs.','It is not a synonym for feeling tired after a run.','concurrent',['Combined demands','Specific outcome','Relevant comparison'],source='concurrent')
term('Protein','Nutrition','A macronutrient made from amino acids, used for structural and functional roles throughout the body, including muscle repair and adaptation.','Food sources include legumes, tofu, eggs, dairy, fish, and meat.','More protein does not substitute for training, enough energy, or a varied diet.','protein',['Dietary protein','Amino acids','Body proteins'],source='protein')
term('Amino acid','Nutrition','A building block of proteins. Essential amino acids must be obtained through the diet because the body cannot make enough to meet its needs.','Different protein foods supply different amino-acid patterns.','A food’s protein content and its amino-acid profile are related but distinct properties.','protein',['Protein food','Amino-acid supply','Body requirements'],source='performance')
term('Protein distribution','Nutrition','How total daily protein is spread across eating occasions. It is separate from the total amount eaten.','112 g could be distributed as four 28 g portions, but equal portions are not mandatory.','Do not turn one idealized meal schedule into a requirement that makes ordinary eating difficult.','protein',['Daily total','Eating occasions','Practical distribution'])
term('Carbohydrate','Nutrition','A broad nutrient category including sugars, starches, and fiber. Different carbohydrate foods also provide different mixtures of other nutrients.','Oats, beans, fruit, and rice all contribute carbohydrate.','Carbohydrate amount alone does not describe a food’s overall quality.','meals',['Sugars','Starches','Fiber'],aliases='carbs')
term('Glycogen','Nutrition','A stored form of carbohydrate found mainly in muscle and liver. Its storage is associated with water, so changes can influence body mass.','A change in carbohydrate intake and training can alter glycogen and water storage.','A rapid scale change is not automatically an equivalent change in fat tissue.','energy',['Stored carbohydrate','Associated water','Variable scale mass'],source='model')
term('Dietary fat','Nutrition','A macronutrient that supplies energy and supports functions including absorption of fat-soluble vitamins. Foods contain different fatty-acid mixtures.','Nuts, olive oil, avocado, and oily fish contribute dietary fat.','Dietary fat and body-fat tissue are not interchangeable concepts.','meals',['Food fats','Energy + nutrients','Dietary pattern'])
term('Macronutrient','Nutrition','A nutrient category consumed in relatively large amounts. In common food tracking, macros refer to protein, carbohydrate, and fat.','A meal can be described by grams of protein, carbohydrate, and fat.','A macro total does not describe all vitamins, minerals, fiber, or food quality.','meals',['Protein','Carbohydrate','Fat'],aliases='macros')
term('Micronutrient','Nutrition','A vitamin or mineral needed in smaller quantities than macronutrients. Small required quantities do not imply small importance.','Iron, calcium, vitamin D, and vitamin B12 are micronutrients.','More is not automatically better; some supplemental amounts can be harmful.','meals',['Vitamins','Minerals','Specific requirements'],aliases='micros')
term('Fiber','Nutrition','Dietary components, largely from plants, that resist digestion in the small intestine and can have varied physiological effects.','Beans, whole grains, fruits, and vegetables can contribute fiber.','Different fibers have different properties; a single label number does not describe them all.','fiber',['Plant food','Nondigested components','Varied properties'],source='fiber',aliases='fibre dietary fiber')
term('Soluble fiber','Nutrition','Fiber that dissolves or disperses in water. Some forms create viscous gels and some are fermented by gut microbes.','Oats and legumes contain soluble fiber alongside other components.','Solubility, viscosity, and fermentability are separate properties, not synonyms.','fiber',['Water interaction','Possible gel formation','Varied fermentation'],source='fiber')
term('Insoluble fiber','Nutrition','Fiber that does not dissolve readily in water. Many plant foods contain both soluble and insoluble fractions.','Whole grains and vegetables can supply insoluble fiber.','The categories overlap in foods; they are not a reason to eat only one type.','fiber',['Plant structure','Low water solubility','Part of mixed foods'],source='fiber')
term('Whole grain','Nutrition','A grain retaining its bran, germ, and endosperm in their original relative proportions, whether intact or processed.','Oats and brown rice are familiar examples.','A dark color or the word “multigrain” does not by itself prove a product is whole grain.','fiber',['Bran','Germ','Endosperm'])
term('Serving size','Nutrition','The reference amount used to state nutrition information on a label. Its meaning depends on the labeling system.','A label may report nutrition per 30 g serving.','It is not automatically the portion you ate or a personalized recommendation.','labels',['Label reference','Nutrients per reference','Scale to intake'],source='label')
term('Portion','Nutrition','The amount of food you actually choose or consume on an occasion. It can differ from the label serving size.','A 45 g portion is 1.5 times a 30 g label serving.','Record the actual amount before multiplying the label values.','labels',['30 g reference','45 g consumed','1.5 servings'])
term('Percent Daily Value','Nutrition','A U.S. label measure showing how a serving contributes to a reference daily nutrient amount. It supports general label comparisons.','A stated %DV applies to the serving shown on that label.','Daily Values are reference amounts, not individual requirements, and other regions use different systems.','labels',['Nutrient in serving','Reference daily amount','Percent contribution'],source='label',aliases='%DV daily value')
term('Energy density','Nutrition','The energy content per unit mass of food, often expressed as kcal per gram or per 100 grams.','240 kcal per 100 g equals 2.4 kcal per gram.','It does not alone describe nutrient quality, fullness, or an appropriate portion.','labels',['240 kcal','100 g food','2.4 kcal/g'])
term('Calorie','Nutrition','In food contexts, “calorie” usually means kilocalorie, abbreviated kcal. It is a unit of energy, not a type of nutrient.','1 kcal equals 4.184 kilojoules.','Check whether an app displays kcal or kJ before comparing values.','energy',['1 kcal','Energy unit','4.184 kJ'],aliases='kcal kilocalorie calories')
term('Energy balance','Nutrition','The relationship between energy intake and energy expenditure over time. Changes in stored body energy are related to the difference.','A persistent imbalance can change energy stores, while daily scale readings also reflect water and contents.','It is a dynamic relationship, not a perfect daily prediction from two app numbers.','energy',['Energy intake','Energy expenditure','Change over time'],source='model')
term('Calorie deficit','Nutrition','A period in which energy intake is lower than energy expenditure. The size is usually estimated rather than directly measured in everyday life.','A planned intake below estimated maintenance is intended to create a deficit.','A target in an app does not prove the actual deficit, and bigger is not automatically appropriate.','energy',['Intake','Below expenditure','Estimated deficit'])
term('Maintenance energy','Nutrition','An estimated energy intake associated with stable body energy stores over a period under given conditions. It changes with circumstances.','A person’s maintenance estimate can differ after changes in body size or activity.','It is not a fixed lifetime calorie number or a guarantee of identical daily scale readings.','energy',['Current body + activity','Observation period','Working estimate'],source='weight',aliases='maintenance calories TDEE')
term('Recipe yield','Nutrition','The total amount or number of portions produced by a recipe. It determines how the recipe’s ingredient totals are divided.','A recipe containing 2,000 kcal divided into four equal portions gives about 500 kcal per portion.','Equal portions by eye may not be equal by weight, and cooking changes water content.','meals',['Ingredient totals','Final recipe yield','Portion share'])
term('Hydration','Nutrition','The state of body water and fluid balance. It is influenced by intake, losses, and health circumstances rather than a single bottle count.','Water, other beverages, and water-containing foods all contribute to intake.','A fixed daily target does not fit every climate, activity level, or medical situation.','hydration',['Fluid intake','Fluid losses','Body-water balance'],source='water')
term('Electrolyte','Nutrition','A substance that forms electrically charged particles in solution. Sodium, potassium, and chloride are familiar examples relevant to body fluids.','Sweat contains water and electrolytes, including sodium.','An electrolyte drink is not automatically necessary for every short workout.','hydration',['Body fluids','Charged particles','Fluid and cell function'])
term('Sodium','Nutrition','A mineral and electrolyte involved in fluid balance and nerve and muscle function. Food labels often list sodium rather than total salt mass.','Sodium chloride is table salt; its sodium content is only part of its mass.','Milligrams of sodium are not the same as milligrams of salt.','hydration',['Sodium','Part of sodium chloride','Distinct label amount'])
term('Sweat rate','Nutrition','An estimate of sweat loss per unit time. Field estimates can use changes in body mass adjusted for fluid consumed and urine produced.','A session estimate is tied to its duration, environment, and activity.','It does not automatically generalize to a different climate or intensity, and measurements contain error.','hydration',['Estimated sweat loss','÷ elapsed time','Loss per hour'])
term('Creatine','Nutrition','A compound involved in rapid energy buffering through the creatine-phosphocreatine system. Creatine monohydrate is the best-studied supplemental form.','It is commonly studied with repeated high-intensity exercise and resistance training.','It is not a stimulant and does not replace training or adequate food intake.','creatine',['Creatine stores','Rapid energy buffering','Repeated hard effort'],source='creatine')
term('Phosphocreatine','Nutrition','A phosphorylated form of creatine that can donate a phosphate to help regenerate ATP during demanding muscular work.','It contributes to rapid ATP replenishment during brief intense efforts.','This energy system overlaps with others; it does not operate alone for an exact fixed number of seconds.','creatine',['Phosphate donation','ADP → ATP','Rapid replenishment'],source='creatine',aliases='PCr')
term('Loading phase','Nutrition','An initial period using a particular higher-intake protocol to increase a substance’s stores more quickly. In creatine use, loading is optional.','A creatine protocol may use loading before a lower maintenance intake.','The term describes a protocol, not a requirement for everyone or permission to improvise large doses.','creatine',['Initial protocol','Store accumulation','Maintenance phase'],source='creatine')
term('Supplement','Nutrition','A product intended to supplement the diet. Ingredients, amounts, evidence, quality, and regulation differ between products and jurisdictions.','A protein powder or creatine product can be a supplement.','A performance claim or high price does not establish safety, purity, or usefulness.','creatine',['Ingredient identity','Actual dose','Evidence + quality'],source='performance')
term('Caffeine','Nutrition','A stimulant found in foods, drinks, medicines, and supplements. Response depends on dose, timing, sensitivity, and circumstances.','Coffee plus an energy drink contributes to the same day’s total intake.','The FDA’s 400 mg adult reference is not a recommended target or a guarantee for everyone.','caffeine',['All sources','Dose + timing','Individual response'],source='caffeine')
term('Recovery','Recovery','The processes and time associated with restoring capacity after training and other demands. Different tissues and performance measures can recover at different rates.','You may feel less sore before a particular performance measure returns to its usual level.','No single score captures every aspect of recovery.','deload',['Training demand','Time + supporting habits','Capacity over time'])
term('Fatigue','Recovery','A reduction in the ability or willingness to perform a task, with multiple possible physical and psychological contributors. It is not one diagnosis.','A usual load may feel harder after a demanding week.','A difficult set does not identify the cause. Consider sleep, illness, schedule, and the task itself.','deload',['Current demand','Reduced task capacity','Possible contributors'])
term('Deload','Recovery','A planned reduction in training demand for a defined period. Programs implement it in different ways.','You reduce working-set count for a busy week and review before rebuilding.','There is no universal percentage or calendar that every trainee needs.','deload',['Usual demand','Planned reduction','Review and return'])
term('DOMS','Recovery','Delayed-onset muscle soreness: discomfort developing after exercise, often after unfamiliar or demanding work. It is not a direct measure of adaptation.','You notice thigh soreness the day after a new exercise variation.','Sharp pain, severe weakness, or unusual symptoms should not automatically be labeled ordinary soreness.','soreness',['Exercise exposure','Later soreness','Assess symptoms'],source='soreness',aliases='delayed onset muscle soreness')
term('Sleep opportunity','Recovery','The amount of time made available for sleep, such as the interval between going to bed and getting up. It is not all necessarily spent asleep.','23:00 to 07:00 provides an eight-hour opportunity.','Awake periods reduce actual sleep time even when the schedule looks adequate.','sleep',['Bedtime','Time available','Wake time'])
term('Sleep duration','Recovery','The amount of time actually spent asleep during a defined period. Self-reports and wearables estimate it in different ways.','Eight hours in bed with awake periods can mean less than eight hours of sleep.','Do not treat time in bed or a device estimate as a perfectly measured duration.','sleep',['Sleep onset','Sleeping intervals','Total sleep time'],source='sleep')
term('Sleep efficiency','Recovery','The proportion of time in bed spent asleep, usually expressed as a percentage. It depends on how both times are measured.','Seven hours asleep during eight hours in bed gives 87.5%.','A high percentage can coexist with too little total sleep opportunity.','sleep',['7 hours asleep','÷ 8 hours in bed','87.5% example'])
term('Circadian rhythm','Recovery','An approximately 24-hour biological rhythm involved in timing many body processes, including the sleep-wake pattern.','Light exposure and habitual timing can influence sleep scheduling.','It is not the same as the amount of sleep pressure accumulated while awake.','sleep',['Biological timing','Environmental cues','Daily pattern'])
term('Cool-down','Recovery','A period of gradually reduced activity after the main session, allowing a more gradual transition toward rest.','After aerobic work, you continue at an easier pace before stopping.','It is not a guarantee against soreness or an excuse to force painful stretching.','warmup',['Main activity','Gradual reduction','Return toward rest'],source='warmup')
term('Mobility','Recovery','A practical term for the ability to access and control movement through a range. It is used differently across coaching settings.','You practice controlled movement through the range needed for a particular exercise.','It is broader than a single flexibility measurement, and more range is not automatically better for every task.','range',['Available range','Control in range','Task requirement'],source='mobility')
term('Training log','Your data','A record of the work performed and the context needed to interpret it. Its usefulness depends on consistency and accurate units.','An exercise entry includes load, repetitions, effort, and relevant setup notes.','A long record with inconsistent conventions can be less useful than a short, repeatable one.','logging',['Work performed','Units + context','Comparable history'])
term('Personal record','Your data','Your best recorded performance on a defined task and standard. The task definition should remain visible.','A best set of ten at a fixed movement standard is a rep-specific record.','Changing equipment, range, or counting conventions can create an apparent record without a fair comparison.','logging',['Defined task','Comparable standard','Best recorded result'],aliases='PR PB personal best')
term('Baseline','Your data','A reference set of observations used for comparison. A useful baseline represents the measurement conditions and period you intend to compare against.','Several comparable morning measurements form a more informative reference than one unusual day.','A baseline is a reference, not a permanent personal norm.','trends',['Reference period','Comparable observations','Future comparison'])
term('Trend','Your data','A pattern of change across multiple observations. Its interpretation depends on noise, missing data, and how the values were collected.','Several weeks of consistent records can show a pattern that one day cannot.','A trend line does not establish why the change happened.','trends',['Repeated observations','Pattern over time','Context for interpretation'])
term('Rolling average','Your data','An average recalculated over a moving window of observations. It smooths short-term variation while lagging some changes.','A seven-day average updates as a new day enters and an old day leaves the window.','Missing values and the choice of window can change the result. State the method.','trends',['Choose window','Average its values','Move the window'])
term('Missing data','Your data','An observation that was not recorded or is unavailable. Its absence is different from a measured zero.','A day without a step record is not necessarily a day with zero steps.','Silently replacing missing values with zero can distort totals and averages.','trends',['No observation','Not a measured zero','Keep the gap visible'])
term('Measurement error','Your data','The difference or uncertainty introduced by a measurement process. It can include systematic bias and random variation.','Device fit or a changed scale position can affect recorded values.','A small difference may reflect the method rather than a meaningful change in the person.','wearables',['Underlying quantity','Measurement process','Recorded value'])
term('Body mass','Your data','The mass measured by a scale, including body tissues, water, and contents. It is not a direct measurement of body fat.','Food, fluid, and glycogen-related changes can affect short-term readings.','Do not interpret each daily change as fat gained or lost.','energy',['Tissues','Water + contents','Scale measurement'],source='model',aliases='weight body weight')
term('Resting heart rate','Your data','Heart rate measured or estimated under resting conditions. Devices may define the daily resting value differently.','A watch-derived resting value may use a different time window from a seated manual measurement.','Compare methods and circumstances before interpreting small changes.','wearables',['Resting conditions','Measurement window','Beats per minute'],source='wearables',aliases='RHR')
term('HRV','Your data','Heart-rate variability: variation in intervals between consecutive heartbeats. Different HRV statistics describe different features and use different units.','A device may report an RMSSD-based value in milliseconds.','It is not the same as heart rate, and higher is not universally better. Do not compare unlike metrics or devices casually.','wearables',['Beat-to-beat intervals','Chosen statistic','Contextual interpretation'],source='hrv',aliases='heart rate variability')
term('Readiness score','Your data','A device- or app-specific composite estimate intended to summarize factors related to readiness. Inputs and weighting vary.','A score may combine recent sleep, activity, and heart-rate information.','It is not a diagnosis or a universally comparable measurement across brands.','wearables',['Selected inputs','Proprietary weighting','Composite estimate'],source='wearables')
term('Correlation','Your data','A statistical description of how variables vary together. It does not by itself establish that one caused the other.','Your better sessions may occur on days with more sleep, but other factors may also differ.','A small personal dataset can show patterns by chance or through confounding factors.','trends',['Variable A','Observed co-variation','Variable B'])
